The vertical shear instability (VSI) involves hydrodynamical processes that likely drive turbulence in the outer regions of protoplanetary disks. However, current models assume a static gas composition and neglect chemical evolution, a key assumption this paper challenges. We demonstrate, utilizing revised disk models with chemical evolution incorporated, that the VSI experiences weakening outerward due to cooling turbulence reducing dust gas coupling efficiency. Specifically, as temperature drops exteriorward, cooling turbulence causes the dust to settle and settle-induced changes in the vertical diffusion coefficient ($\alpha_{
u}$), modifying it such that $\alpha_{
u}$ exceeds the stable value ($\alpha_{
u} > \sigma_{\sigma}$) over an broader radial range. While this potentially extends the VSI's operational radius, the increased diffusion simultaneously elevates the critical threshold ($\alpha_{\sigma}$) for onset, ultimately resulting in a net reduction of the maximum $\alpha$ achievable before the system stabilizes. Our findings suggest chemical evolution plays a pivotal in regulating turbulence and may help explain observed low accretion rates in the outer disk.
